e Time: 28.4" [:br {}] "Width: 19.0 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The competitor to beat in the men’s moguls was Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/127471"} "Mikaël Kingsbury"] ", who had taken silver in this event in 2014 and gold in 2018. He was also a six-time World Champion, including at the last edition in 2021. The 2018 Olympic runner-up, " [:a {:href "/athletes/127299"} "Matt Graham"] " of Australia, was also in contention, while bronze medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/137451"} "Daichi Hara"] " of Japan was competing as well. The most recent world runner-up was France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/127745"} "Ben Cavet"] ", with " [:a {:href "/athletes/128053"} "Pasha Kolmakov"] " of Kazakhstan having rounded out that podium."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Kingsbury and Cavet came up first and third in the first qualifying round, with Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/138177"} "Walter Wallberg"] ", who had been 21st in 2018, was second. Wallberg was a surprise qualifier, as he had never won a World Cup event and had nearly quit the sport following a series of injuries a few years earlier. It was therefore all the more surprising when Wallberg came out on top after the third round of the final with a score of 83.23 compared to Kingsbury’s 82.18. Cavet, meanwhile, just missed the podium in fourth, with Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/137452"} "Ikuma Horishima"] ", the 2017 World Champion who had been 11th in 2018, taking bronze. Wallberg’s victory, a first ever in the sport for Sweden, dethroned Canada as the champions in this event, as they had not lost gold since 2006."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying Round 1"] [:th {} "Qualifying Round 2"] [:th {} "Final Round 1"] [:th {} "Final Round 2"] [:th {} "Final Round 3"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes